# enterprise-linux -- USBGuard baseline rules (layer 8). GENERATED FILE.
# Source: security/hardening/data/usbguard-rules.template
# Rendered by el-usbguard-rules --storage @STORAGE_POLICY@
#
# Evaluation is first-match. "equals { ... }" requires the device's whole
# interface set to match, so composite devices that hide e.g. a mass-storage
# or network interface behind a keyboard ("BadUSB") do NOT match an allow rule
# and fall through to the implicit block.
#
# Known trade-off: any device that presents itself purely as a keyboard is
# allowed (keystroke-injection tools included). Mitigation later: user
# approval prompts for new HID devices via eld + usbguard-notifier.

# --- Removable storage policy: @STORAGE_POLICY@ ---
@STORAGE_RULES@

# --- Always allowed classes ---
# Hubs
allow with-interface equals { 09:*:* }
# Keyboards, mice, touchpads, FIDO2/U2F security keys (HID)
allow with-interface equals { 03:*:* }
# Smartcard readers and YubiKey-style HID+CCID composites (PIV login)
allow with-interface equals { 0b:*:* }
allow with-interface equals { 03:*:* 0b:*:* }
# Audio (headsets: audio + HID volume buttons)
allow with-interface equals { 01:*:* }
allow with-interface equals { 01:*:* 03:*:* }
# Video (webcams, often with a microphone)
allow with-interface equals { 0e:*:* }
allow with-interface equals { 0e:*:* 01:*:* }
# Printers
allow with-interface equals { 07:*:* }
# Bluetooth controllers (wireless controller class)
allow with-interface equals { e0:*:* }
# USB network adapters (CDC ECM/NCM) used by docks and dongles
allow with-interface equals { 02:*:* 0a:*:* }

# --- Explicitly allowed devices (docks etc.) from policy ---
@ALLOW_IDS@
